import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'..')
from par_helpers import *
save(3,"The Parent’s Own Mind","What is the early sign in my own body, the minute before the old recording plays, and what could a short repair sound like in my family?",[
mc("U","According to Lesson 3, why does an old reaction tend to play at certain moments and not all day?",
 ["It plays when a child behaves worse than usual, because the child’s conduct sets it off.","It plays when the parent has practised too little, so the habit has grown strong again.","It plays when tiredness, hurry or worry shrinks the gap, and the reaction arrives first.","It plays when the parent’s true feelings finally break through after being held back."],2,
 "The gap between something arriving and our deciding what it means is where everything useful happens. Tiredness, hurry, hunger, worry and a long day shrink it. When the gap is a hair’s width, the old recording plays before you have arrived. It is a timing problem, not a character problem.",
 "Tiredness and hurry shrink the gap, so the old recording plays first","Part 1"),
mc("A","After snapping at a child, a parent says to herself, “That was my mother’s voice. I couldn’t help it.” Which view fits Lesson 3?",
 ["It is an honest account, so there is nothing further to look at.","Naming where it comes from only gives the habit more strength, so it is better left unsaid.","It explains the snap, which is where change starts; it is not an excuse.","It shows she should resolve never to raise her voice again."],2,
 "It is not an excuse. It is an explanation, and an explanation is where change starts. “I couldn’t help it” ends the conversation. “I can see where it comes from” opens one. A path laid down in childhood is not a character flaw, and nobody chooses the first paths they walk.",
 "An explanation, not an excuse: seeing where the reaction comes from","Part 1"),
judge("“When a parent hears her mother’s voice coming out of her own mouth, the part of her that heard it was caught in it as well.”",F,
 "Vedanta speaks of the witness, the awareness in which every thought, mood and old voice appears and passes. That awareness was there too, and it is what heard the voice and knew it was her mother’s. So the lesson says not to fight the recording but to strengthen the one who hears it.",
 "The witness hears the recording and is not caught in it","Part 1"),
mc("U","What does the lesson mean in saying that a reaction swinging away from a pattern is often still drawn by it?",
 ["A parent who does the opposite of her own parent has, in fact, broken the pattern.","“Never like her” keeps that parent at the centre of every decision.","Doing the opposite is usually harmless, because it takes the harm out of the old pattern.","A pattern that is swung away from will only come back stronger in the next generation."],1,
 "A reaction that swings away is like a pendulum. “I will never be like her” keeps her at the centre of every decision, and the Gītā counts aversion as a bond just as strong as attachment. Freedom is not becoming the opposite of your mother. It is choosing from what your daughter needs.",
 "Aversion binds as attachment does; freedom is choosing from what the child needs","Part 1"),
mc("A","A parent feels her shoulders rise as a child cannot find the worksheet for the third time. Which step fits Lesson 3 at that moment?",
 ["Push the feeling down firmly, so that nothing of it shows.","Say everything she is feeling at once, so that nothing is held back.","Take one slow breath, longer on the way out, then ask what the child’s mind needs.","Wait until the child has left the room, then work out what she ought to have said."],2,
 "Notice, pause, choose. The pause is the widening of the gap: one breath with a longer out-breath, a sip of water, a step back, or the sentence “Let me think for a moment.” Then, from inside the gap, ask what the child’s mind needs and what she wants to say.",
 "Notice, pause, choose: the pause widens the gap","Part 2"),
mc("A","A parent whose own mother was strict and cold finds she cannot say no, and then blows up. Which step fits Lesson 3?",
 ["Say no sternly each time she is asked, until the habit of saying yes has gone away for good.","Say yes to small things, so that a no can be kept for the big matters.","Explain each no in full, so that her daughter always understands the reason.","Practise a warm no: “I love you, and the answer is no.”"],3,
 "The pendulum swings between “never again” and a blow-up. The lesson suggests lending yourself the middle: a warm no. It is allowed to be warm and firm in the same sentence, and that is the seed to plant beside both of the old ones.",
 "The warm no: warm and firm in the same sentence","Part 2"),
mc("A","A parent shouted at a nine-year-old in front of friends and has now calmed down. Which repair fits Lesson 3?",
 ["Wait for the child to raise it, since raising it would only reopen the hurt for the child.","Say what happened, say sorry plainly, say what she will try next, and then let it go.","Explain how hard the day had been and why she was so short, so the child understands.","Say sorry, and ask the child to say that it is okay so that the matter can be closed."],1,
 "Repair is very short. Wait until you are calm and the child is calm, get close, say what happened without excuses, say you are sorry in plain words, say what you will try next time, and then let it go. Do not ask the child to comfort you. The repair is for the relationship, not a request for absolution.",
 "A short repair: what happened, sorry, what next, then let it go","Part 3"),
mc("U","According to Lesson 3, what does a mistake followed by repair teach a child that a perfect parent could not?",
 ["That people who love each other can get it wrong and come back.","That parents are always fair, even when they raise their voices.","That strong feelings are best kept quiet until everyone is calm.","That mistakes are forgotten soonest when nobody mentions them."],0,
 "Children learn from what we do, and also from how we mend. A mistake followed by repair teaches that people who love each other can get it wrong and come back. Repair is part of good parenting, not a sign that it has failed, and a repair years overdue can still be made.",
 "Repair is part of good parenting, not a sign it has failed","Part 3"),
judge("“Because a child draws patience and tenderness out of a parent, the child is partly responsible for the parent’s growth and happiness.”",F,
 "The child is not a tool for anyone’s growth and is never responsible for a parent’s happiness. The child has their own journey. What happens is on the parent’s side, quietly, as love is practised daily. A light thank-you to a child is fine, as long as it asks nothing back.",
 "The child is the parent’s teacher without carrying any responsibility for the parent","Part 4"),
mc("A","At six in the evening a parent is making supper and doing homework and the bath. What does Lesson 3’s practical Vedanta change about this?",
 ["Nothing in the tasks; the change is in seeing the child as a guest to be served.","The tasks themselves, which can be fewer once the parent sees them as a spiritual practice.","The child’s part in the evening, since a child can be asked to help with the offering of the meal.","The timing of supper, which should wait until the parent has first sat quietly alone."],0,
 "Nothing dramatic changes: the meal, the bath, the homework and the lift stay the same. What changes is the way of looking at it. Feeding the child becomes an offering, and not only a chore. It is harder to reach for the old recording when you are looking at a guest and not a problem.",
 "The tasks stay the same; the way of looking changes: serving the Divine in the child","Part 4")])
